WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:05.920
After a sweet debut in Jessica Jones, Mike
Colter's Luke Cage powers back on to Netflix

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.850
with his very own series.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:12.089
And just like Daredevil and Jessica Jones
before him, Luke Cage's show is bursting with

00:00:12.089 --> 00:00:16.970
easter eggs, cameos and references to everything
from Star Wars to Iron Fist, Deadpool, and

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:18.910
a stack of comic books.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:22.790
I've put together as many of those easter
eggs and call-backs as I could in this video.

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:24.850
Do let me know what other cool ones you spotted!

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:28.390
And just a quick reminder, of course, there
are spoilers ahead!

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.770
Luke Cage first appeared in issue 1 of Hero
For Hire, which is describes how his character

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:38.200
operated and is one of his aliases, so it's
not surprising that there are several nods

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.700
to that in the Netflix show.

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:45.150
After Luke saves Genghis Connie's from Cottonmouth's
men, Connie says she wants to hire Luke; however,

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:50.260
in contrast to the comics, Luke says he's
not for hire but he will look out for her.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.020
And when Luke tells Fish 'I ain't no hero',
during their conversation in the diner, to

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.850
which Fish replies, 'that's a great slogan.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:01:00.860
I ain't no hero; pay me!', that's a nice reference
to the fact that in the comics, Luke's heroic

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.910
activities were available for a fee.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:07.200
Method Man also gives a shout-out to Luke's
"Hero For Hire" alias in his rap "Bulletproof

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.200
Love".

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.160
From issue 17, the "Hero For Hire" comic book
series was titled "Power Man" when Luke decided

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:14.750
he needed a better name.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.060
And there are a number of hints to his Power
Man moniker in the show.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.320
Pop jokingly calls Luke "Power Man" on a couple
of occasions, and when Claire Temple calls

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.900
Luke "Macho Man", only to be corrected by
Luke who says his name is "Power Man", she

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:28.730
laughs that that's even cornier.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:33.439
And there's a nod to the "Ace of Spades" alias
which Luke rejected in the comics when Luke's

00:01:33.439 --> 00:01:38.860
told to take up six bottles of Ace of Spades
to the club's VIP area during the first episode.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.870
The silver metal headgear and gauntlets we
see Luke wearing following Dr Burstein's experiment

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.479
is a call-back to the character's original
comic-book costume which he donned for many

00:01:47.479 --> 00:01:50.950
years following his first appearance in 1972.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:55.610
And Luke's comic-book headgear gets another
mention later in the show when Luke and Claire

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:59.079
Temple discover a file on Reva's zip-drive
with the title 'Tiara'.

00:01:59.079 --> 00:02:03.270
By the way, Tiara was also the production
codename for the Netflix series.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.560
Luke's traditional comic-book look also included
a partially open yellow shirt, blue trousers

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.730
and a chain belt, all of which make an appearance
in the show when he grabs a yellow shirt and

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:16.750
a pair of blue jeans from a washing line after
his escape from prison.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:20.380
When Luke catches sight of his reflection
and comments that his new clothes make him

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:24.510
look like 'a damn fool', it's a nice reference
to the fact that in the comics he also felt

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.450
his superhero outfit was 'kinda hokey'.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:32.200
And just as Luke drops the 70s-style clothes
for more 21st-century appropriate attire in

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.230
the show after that, his comic book character
destroyed his traditional costume in 1992

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.569
on the cover of the first issue of the new
series Cage.

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:45.220
The T-shirts, jeans, and casual jackets Luke
wears throughout the show reflect his more

00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:49.030
modern look in the comic books from the 1990s
onwards.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:53.400
Luke's hoodie is a nod to 17-year-old Trayvon
Martin, who was shot dead by George Zimmerman

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.220
in Florida in 2012.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:59.660
And showrunner Cheo Coker has said that it
was important to him, 'as the father of two

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:03.790
black sons, to have a black man in a hoodie
and have them not be a threat.'

00:03:03.790 --> 00:03:07.720
The colour of Luke's hoodie references his
comic book costumes from his early days through

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:12.070
to more contemporary times as does the blue
tracksuit and yellow bandage worn by the young

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:16.080
Carl Lucas training in a boxing gym during
a flashback scene.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.390
And Luke's red T-shirt in the show is a call-back
to the 90s Cage comics, where Luke dropped

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:22.770
his previous yellow outfit for red.

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:26.489
The Incident, aka The Battle of New York,
and the Avengers get a number of mentions

00:03:26.489 --> 00:03:27.489
in the show.

00:03:27.489 --> 00:03:31.060
For example, there's a lad selling raw footage
of the Incident on DVD, which he says features

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.370
Tony Stark, the 'big blond dude with the hammer',
'the old dude with the shield', and 'the green

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.409
monster', in other words Thor, Captain America,
and the Hulk.

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:42.560
Then there's Claire Temple's mother who talks
about the way the world's changed with 'aliens

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:46.390
coming out of the sky', 'people with silver
hammers', and 'green monsters', again referring

00:03:46.390 --> 00:03:49.340
to Thor and the Hulk, as well as the Chitauri
invasion.

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:53.450
After being explored most recently in Captain
America: Civil War, as well as Daredevil and

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:58.019
Jessica Jones, the MCU's continuing big debate
about whether superheroes are a good thing

00:03:58.019 --> 00:04:02.690
to be supported or whether their powers should
be controlled crops up repeatedly in Luke

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:03.690
Cage.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.860
Early on in the show, Misty Knight and her
partner Scarfe are on opposite sides of the

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:07.860
issue.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.180
Scarfe, who says he saw the Incident up close
and what cops can and can't do, is happy for

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:14.250
people like Cage to clean up the streets.

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:19.250
While Misty believes it's anarchy and that
superheroes have no responsibility or training.

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:23.939
Iron Man 2 villain Justin Hammer appears to
be still in business as we see Hammer Industries

00:04:23.939 --> 00:04:28.620
weapons being sold by Cottonmouth, and the
Judas bullet that Diamondback uses against

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:32.790
Luke is said to be based on a prototype by
Hammer and is made from alien metal from the

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:33.790
Incident.

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:38.159
Interestingly, in the Marvel One-Shot All
Hail The King we discovered that Justin Hammer

00:04:38.159 --> 00:04:43.370
was incarcerated in Seagate prison, just as
Luke Cage had been, which also links to Reva's

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.659
comment in the show about "millionaires hidden
in dungeons" at Seagate.

00:04:47.659 --> 00:04:51.879
When Misty Knight injures her arm during the
hostage siege at Harlem's Paradise, it's a

00:04:51.879 --> 00:04:56.050
nod to the bionic arm that Tony Stark gave
her in the comics after she lost her own arm

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.840
during a bomb attack.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:01.610
Misty's hairstyle and the red dress she's
wearing in the last shot we see her in the

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:05.919
final episode is a reference to her classic
red costume and look in the comics.

00:05:05.919 --> 00:05:10.050
Simone Missick has confirmed Misty Knight
is returning for The Defenders so the show

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:14.520
could be hinting that we might see her suited
up next time and with a possible super-powered

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:15.520
arm.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:20.199
The Biggie Smalls portrait by Barron Claiborne
that hangs on Cottonmouth's wall is a nod

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:25.409
to Luke Cage showrunner Cheo Coker who wrote
a biography of the Notorious B.I.G. and who

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:27.529
also co-wrote his biopic Notorious.

00:05:27.529 --> 00:05:33.059
And in the show's second episode, Faith Evans
who is Biggie's widow also performs the song

00:05:33.059 --> 00:05:39.300
Mesmerized at Harlem's Paradise during a discussion
Mariah and Cornell have in front of the portrait.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:44.139
Marvel opted against giving Cornell "Cottonmouth"
Stokes the sharp fang-like teeth his character

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:48.629
has in the comics, however there's a hint
at that when Pop talks about how Cottonmouth

00:05:48.629 --> 00:05:50.469
lost three teeth as a child.

00:05:50.469 --> 00:05:55.289
The show's costume designer, Stephanie Maslansky,
said she wanted to reflect Cottonmouth's snake-like

00:05:55.289 --> 00:05:59.749
green colours from the comics, which is why
she had Mahershala Ali wear what she calls

00:05:59.749 --> 00:06:04.509
a "fantastic, fly-looking green suit" the
first time we see Cornell Stokes.

00:06:04.509 --> 00:06:08.539
When Luke Cage calls Diamondback a snake,
it's a nod to the villain's snakeskin-like

00:06:08.539 --> 00:06:09.969
costume from the comics.

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:14.300
And in the show, Diamondback's Hammer Tech
costume does resemble the character's comic-book

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.499
costume, except for the helmet.

00:06:16.499 --> 00:06:21.569
When Diamondback reveals his true relationship
to Luke, with the words "I am your brother",

00:06:21.569 --> 00:06:25.520
it calls to mind Darth Vader's reveal "I am
your father" reveal to Luke Skywalker in The

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:27.300
Empire Strikes Back.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.469
Star Wars gets another couple of references
with Pop talking about Jedi mind tricks and

00:06:31.469 --> 00:06:35.990
Fish calling Diamondback a pimp Stormtrooper
when he arrives at the barbershop in his Hammer

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:37.399
Tech suit.

00:06:37.399 --> 00:06:41.860
And the show continues the tradition of Marvel's
Phase 2 homage to Star Wars limb loss, with

00:06:41.860 --> 00:06:46.309
Mabel Stokes severing the finger of a young
lad who's started selling drugs.

00:06:46.309 --> 00:06:50.539
Although Willis Stryker, aka Diamondback,
and Carl Lucas were never brothers in the

00:06:50.539 --> 00:06:51.759
comics, they were friends.

00:06:51.759 --> 00:06:55.749
In both the show and the comics, Willis was
jealous of Carl and framed him for a crime

00:06:55.749 --> 00:07:00.839
he didn't commit which led him to Seagate
prison, however, the comics and TV show portray

00:07:00.839 --> 00:07:03.669
Willis's jealousy and Carl's crime differently.

00:07:03.669 --> 00:07:08.210
Still the name of the Judas bullet which Diamondback
uses against Luke fits this whole idea of

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.309
betrayal.

00:07:09.309 --> 00:07:13.080
When Diamondback pretends he was responsible
for Reva's death in the show, it's a nod to

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:17.580
the comics where he did accidentally cause
her death after she was caught in mob crossfire

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:18.849
that was targeting Willis.

00:07:18.849 --> 00:07:24.089
There's a sneaky easter egg to Timely Comics
on the dumpster labelled Timely Trash behind

00:07:24.089 --> 00:07:26.559
Diamondback and Misty Knight in episode 8.

00:07:26.559 --> 00:07:31.419
Timely Trash's logo is similar to that of
Timely Comics, which was founded in 1939 and

00:07:31.419 --> 00:07:33.129
later became Marvel Comics.

00:07:33.129 --> 00:07:37.679
There's a name drop to the supervillain Black
Mariah when Cornell calls his cousin Mariah

00:07:37.679 --> 00:07:39.159
Dillard by that name.

00:07:39.159 --> 00:07:42.949
Black Mariah was a Luke Cage enemy in the
comics who later became involved in the drug

00:07:42.949 --> 00:07:47.729
trade so the name drop could indicate Dillard's
possible future in the Netflix shows.

00:07:47.729 --> 00:07:52.939
Many of the scenes at Seagate are rather faithful
to Luke Cage's origin story as told in Hero

00:07:52.939 --> 00:07:54.270
for Hire #1.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.929
The sadistic and racist prison guard Albert
Rackham who triggered Luke Cage's superpowers

00:07:58.929 --> 00:08:03.689
by deliberately overheating Luke's experimental
chemical bath has a very similar comic book

00:08:03.689 --> 00:08:05.800
counterpart called "Billy Bob" Rackham.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.289
In Hero for Hire, Rackham also deliberately
tries to kill Carl Lucas by turning the dial

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:11.830
up on the experiment.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:15.659
The way Lucas discovers his super strength
after punching the prison wall, and the way

00:08:15.659 --> 00:08:20.330
he punches through to escape from Seagate
is also remarkably close to the comic book

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:22.289
pages from Hero for Hire.

00:08:22.289 --> 00:08:27.550
A poster behind Reva for a talk by Warden
Stuart is a shout-out to Warden Tyler Stuart

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:32.080
from the comics who moved Rackham down the
ranks to a regular guard at Seagate.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.479
Shades also spent time in Seagate with Luke
Cage and has a similar story in the comics,

00:08:36.479 --> 00:08:38.769
later returning to New York to a life of crime.

00:08:38.769 --> 00:08:44.000
Interestingly, in the comics Shades also works
against Luke Cage and Iron Fist, but for Ward

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:49.300
Meachum who upgrades Shades' sunglasses to
an optic blasting visor, which is something

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:52.820
we could see in the upcoming Iron Fist show
or The Defenders.

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:57.649
Dr Burstein was also at Seagate conducting
experiments on inmates in both the comics

00:08:57.649 --> 00:08:58.649
and show.

00:08:58.649 --> 00:09:02.230
In the comics, he experimented with a version
of the super-soldier serum used to create

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:07.149
Captain America and although that's not stated
explicitly in in the show, there is an allusion

00:09:07.149 --> 00:09:11.660
to it when Cottonmouth sarcastically asks
Luke if he thinks he's Harlem's Captain America.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:16.100
And when Dr Burstein later offers Claire Temple
a job working with him, it's a nod to the

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:19.579
time in the comics when Burstein and Claire
worked together at the Storefront Clinic in

00:09:19.579 --> 00:09:20.790
New York.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:24.829
And the end-of-season kiss between Claire
and Luke reflects the fact that Claire Temple

00:09:24.829 --> 00:09:28.500
was also been one of Luke Cage's earliest
love interests in the comics.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:33.949
Like the previous Netflix Marvel shows, Luke
Cage continues to show parallels between Claire

00:09:33.949 --> 00:09:37.759
and the comic book character Night Nurse,
for example, during the Harlem's Paradise

00:09:37.759 --> 00:09:41.920
hostage situation when Claire fakes having
to leave to get some medicine, one of the

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:45.820
guards says "Escort the night nurse here downstairs".

00:09:45.820 --> 00:09:50.020
When Luke accidentally destroys a can of shaving
cream after his prison escape, it's a nod

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:54.990
to the 2-page "Magic Shave" Luke Cage custom
comic ad by Marvel in which Luke accidentally

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:57.810
interrupts a guy who's using "Magic Shave"
cream.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:01.270
In the ad, Luke accidentally smashes through
the bathroom wall during a fight with the

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:06.160
Absorbing Man and quips he didn't even get
scratched, a good excuse for the shaving guy

00:10:06.160 --> 00:10:10.269
to explain how he didn't get scratched either
thanks to "Magic Shave".

00:10:10.269 --> 00:10:14.930
We get to hear Luke Cage say his classic catchphrase
from the comics "Sweet Christmas" five times

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:21.079
in the show as well as a young Willis Stryker
telling a young Luke during boxing training

00:10:21.079 --> 00:10:23.339
"not to give him that Sweet Christmas shit".

00:10:23.339 --> 00:10:28.120
According to comic book writer and Static
Shock creator Dwayne McDuffie, Luke's catchphrase

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:32.891
owes its existence to the fact that Luke Cage
writer Archie Goodwin took inspiration from

00:10:32.891 --> 00:10:35.220
the Harlem Detective novels of author Chester
Himes.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.209
And actually, there's a couple of shout-outs
specifically to Chester Himes in the show

00:10:39.209 --> 00:10:43.069
both at Pop's Barber Shop and when Luke is
discussing books while being taken back to

00:10:43.069 --> 00:10:44.740
prison in the finale.

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:48.430
Another book that makes an appearance in the
show is Walter Mosley's Little Green, which

00:10:48.430 --> 00:10:50.290
we see Luke reading at various points.

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:54.730
The hardboiled detective novel is part of
Mosley's Easy Rawlins series and has Easy

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:58.660
waking up after falling into a coma in the
previous book and features the detective's

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:00.430
search for a missing young man.

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.759
This parallels Luke Cage's situation in the
show where he's recovered from being shot

00:11:03.759 --> 00:11:06.709
in the head in Jessica Jones and is trying
to find Chico.

00:11:06.709 --> 00:11:10.899
There's a cool tease for Iron Fist in the
finale when Claire takes the number off a

00:11:10.899 --> 00:11:15.269
flyer advertising self-defence and martial
arts training by Sensei Colleen Wing.

00:11:15.269 --> 00:11:20.029
Colleen Wing will be one of Danny Rand's allies
in the next Netflix Marvel series Iron Fist

00:11:20.029 --> 00:11:22.720
and will be played by Jessica Henwick.

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:26.699
And the upcoming Defenders series gets plenty
more set-up in Luke Cage with references to

00:11:26.699 --> 00:11:30.060
Daredevil and Jessica Jones, the other key
members of the group.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:33.810
In the finale, Claire offers to contact a
really great lawyer she knows, referencing

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.069
Matt Murdock, aka Daredevil.

00:11:36.069 --> 00:11:40.180
And after Fish finds the missing files that
can prove Luke's innocence, presumably Matt

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:42.920
Murdock will be able to appeal his sentence.

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:46.819
Jessica Jones and how she killed Kilgrave
gets referenced in Mariah Dillard's speech

00:11:46.819 --> 00:11:51.180
against super-powered individuals where she
mentions how "that woman over in Hell's Kitchen

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:54.100
snapped a man's neck because he was mind-controlling
her."

00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:59.230
And Trish Walker, Jessica Jones's best friend,
makes a little voice cameo in Luke Cage as

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:02.889
we hear her radio show Trish Talk discussing
Luke Cage's activities.

00:12:02.889 --> 00:12:09.889
The lawyer who represents Cottonmouth and
VIP hostess Candace is Ben Donovan, who also

00:12:09.889 --> 00:12:13.139
acted as Wilson Fisk's lawyer in Season 2
of Daredevil.

00:12:13.139 --> 00:12:17.939
His comic book counterpart is "Big" Ben Donovan,
a Harlem-based lawyer who first appeared in

00:12:17.939 --> 00:12:20.509
1973 in the Hero for Hire series.

00:12:20.509 --> 00:12:25.180
For a time, in the comics, Donovan acted as
a lawyer for both Cage and Claire Temple.

00:12:25.180 --> 00:12:29.470
Assistant DA Blake Tower from the second season
of Daredevil also makes a brief cameo when

00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:33.249
he arrives to check on the hostage situation
at Harlem's Paradise.

00:12:33.249 --> 00:12:37.649
While Stan Lee popped up in police station
posters and pictures in Daredevil and Jessica

00:12:37.649 --> 00:12:41.889
Jones, in Luke Cage his face features on a
poster in a local store window.

00:12:41.889 --> 00:12:45.999
And right below that poster, there's another
one advertising a Nightclub Party with the

00:12:45.999 --> 00:12:51.490
word DJ in big letters, which feels like a
little hint at Lee's cameo as a strip club

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:54.249
DJ or MC in the Deadpool movie.

00:12:54.249 --> 00:12:57.740
Method Man makes a cool cameo in the store
scene with two bumbling robbers.

00:12:57.740 --> 00:13:02.600
He jokes with Luke Cage who gives a shout-out
to his early work and they swap hoodies.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:06.779
Later Method Man performs his Luke Cage tribute
rap "Bulletproof Love" on the real-life radio

00:13:06.779 --> 00:13:10.260
show Sway's Universe Sway In The Morning.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:13.830
Interestingly, one of Method Man's aliases
is Johnny Blaze, which is also the name of

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:18.089
one of Marvel's Ghost Rider characters, who
was played on the big screen by superhero

00:13:18.089 --> 00:13:21.990
fan Nic Cage, who took his surname from Luke
Cage!

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:26.270
There's numerous nods to the raised fist salute
that is often associated with the Black Panther

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:28.220
Party and the Black Power movement.

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:32.790
There's Luke Cage's raised fist as it punches
out of the rubble of Genghis Connie's in episode

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.889
4 and the fist Luke makes while he's being
treated in Burstein's tank of boiling acid

00:13:36.889 --> 00:13:38.149
in episode 9.

00:13:38.149 --> 00:13:42.750
By the way Black Lives Matter also crops up
in a speech by Mariah Dillard.

00:13:42.750 --> 00:13:49.670
The Luke Cage show introduces a new TV news
network WJBP-TV to the Marvel Cinematic Universe

